The below images show how this program is stored in the call stack and how it is executed.Stack OverflowSince the call stack has limited memory, repeatedly creating stack frames without removing them can exhaust the available stack space and cause a stack overflow. This commonly occurs due to uncontrolled recursion.
            C++

    #include <iostream> 
using namespace std; 
void fun() { 
    fun(); 

} 

int main() { 
    fun(); 
    return 0; 

}

In the above program, the function continuously calls itself without a stopping condition. As new stack frames keep getting added, the call stack eventually runs out of memory, resulting in a stack overflow.
